WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.917
This query writes the cumulative frame explicitly.

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:07.867
It starts at the first row of the ordered partition and ends at the current row.

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.772
Writing the frame makes the intended behavior visible to the reader.

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:19.462
That is especially useful in teaching and in production queries where the frame meaning matters.

00:00:19.462 --> 00:00:22.602
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:28.787
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:31.357
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:31.357 --> 00:00:37.892
In a real setting such as an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event,

00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:43.647
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:45.772
before discussing syntax.

00:00:45.772 --> 00:00:51.680
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
